<mujoco model="so101_tower_stand">
  <!-- THE OVERHEAD CAMERA STAND OF THE `so101-tower` RIG — a STATIC slot.

       The four printed parts of the SO-ARM100 repo's
       `Optional/Overhead_Cam_Mount_32x32_UVC_Module` (arm_base,
       cam_mount_bottom, cam_mount_middle, cam_mount_top), assembled, with
       the IMX335 camera at the far end of `cam_mount_top`. The follower arm
       is bolted onto the `arm_base` plate, which is what fixes the
       arm-to-overhead-camera transform by construction
       (`docs/camera-rig.md` §1, §5).

       ⚠⚠ THE ASSEMBLY IS DERIVED FROM THE STLs, NOT MEASURED ON THE RIG.
       The parts are authored in ONE assembly frame two at a time:
       `cam_mount_middle` (y 35..198 mm) and `cam_mount_top` (y 190..372)
       overlap by the 8 mm push-fit and share a frame; `cam_mount_bottom`
       (y 0..231) is its own, its top 8 mm (y 223..231) already the middle's
       25.4 x 36.6 section centred at x 18.7, z 36.5. So middle/top sit in
       the bottom's frame at (+18.7, +188, +36.5) mm. `arm_base` CHAINS ONTO
       THE FOOT END TO END, along z: both parts end in a 37.4 mm-wide,
       10 mm finger-joint tab (the foot at z -10..0 and 75..83, the plate at
       z -133..-120 and 15..26 — a bimanual rig chains plate, foot, plate),
       so the plate keeps the foot's orientation and its z = 26 tongue meets
       the foot's z = 0: translation (+94, 0, -26) mm, the plate at x 0..90,
       z -159..0. The first two versions had it behind the tower (its
       x = -4.3 lip read as a tab) and then rotated 90° along the forward
       axis — the rig's owner corrected both against the real rig. Every
       one of those is a       few-mm assumption a push-fit can move, which is why
       `camera-rig.md` §5 says: CALIBRATE (§7), and treat these as the
       starting values. `projects/so101-tower/calibration/` is where the
       calibrated pose goes.

       ⚠⚠ THE PLATE NORMAL IS IN THE TOWER'S x-y PLANE, NOT y-z. The doc's §5
       wrote the top plate's normal as (0, -0.4242, 0.9056) — the WRIST
       plate's convention. Recomputed from `cam_mount_top.stl`, the two
       largest faces of the bent head are (0.42, -0.91, 0) at (28.9, 362.0, 0)
       and (-0.42, 0.91, 0) at (28.1, 364.9, 0), 3.0 mm apart: the camera
       plate faces DOWN and FORWARD along the bend, 65° below horizontal.
       That is what an overhead camera at 54 cm sees the workspace with —
       the optical axis meets the desk ~30 cm in front of the tower — and it
       is what the recorded overhead frames show (the tower's foot at the
       bottom edge of the image, the bowl near the centre). The doc's
       numbers would have looked 25° below horizontal at the far wall.

       FRAMES. The STLs are y-up, mm; this body is z-up, m. Every mesh geom
       carries `quat="0.7071068 0.7071068 0 0"` (+90° about x: part y -> z,
       part z -> -y) and `scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"`. Body +x is the head's
       bend direction = the arm's forward (+x); body +y is the arm's left.

       THE ARM SITS AT body (+0.019, +0.077, +0.005): the plate's hole
       pattern (x -88..-18, z -22..-80 in its frame) lands at body x 6..76,
       y 48..106, and the SO-101 base body origin sits 22 mm behind its
       footprint centre, facing +x; on top of the 5 mm plate. So the arm is
       IN LINE with the foot (its origin 0.3 mm off the foot's centre line)
       and 7.7 cm to the tower's left (the arm's +y). The family places this
       slot at the negation of that so the
       robot stays at the world origin.

       THE OVERHEAD CAMERA: plate face centre (28.94, 361.98, 0) in the top's
       frame, outward normal (0.4243, -0.9055, 0); entrance pupil 15.6 mm
       along it (adapter 3 + standoffs 5 + PCB 1.6 + ~6 to the pupil, §4;
       19.0 measured since — the bake's `PUPIL_ALONG_NORMAL_MM` — and the
       calibrated pose below supersedes the whole derivation);
       plus the (18.7, 188, 36.5) frame offset; y-up -> z-up. Image-up is
       FORWARD (the far edge of the desk at the top of the recorded frames),
       image-right is the robot's right (-y). `fovy` is the virtual pinhole
       the fisheye frames are undistorted to — 90° horizontal at 4:3, see
       `tools/soarm/bake_so_arm101_tower.py`.

       ⚠⚠ THE CAMERA POSE IS NOW THE CALIBRATED ONE (2026-09-25), not the
       STL derivation above, which put it at pos (0.05426, -0.0365, 0.53585),
       xyaxes (0 -1 0  0.9055 0 0.4243). Fitted from 62 marker captures of
       `calibrate_camera_extrinsics.mojo` (projects/so101-tower/cameras/
       camera_overhead*.poses.txt, 2026-09-22 + 2026-09-25, nothing moved
       between them): gripper FK from the raw ticks through the measured
       zero (roll +5.0 included), the marker fixed at its photographed place
       on the camera plate, the camera free. The derivation was off by
       (+6.4, -3.5, +0.8) mm and 0.55 deg (leave-one-out sd 2-3 mm); the
       ~9 mm camera-vs-arm offset the pose estimator saw was this.

       COLLISION is four boxes: the foot, the post, the head and the base
       plate. ⚠ THE POST IS INSIDE THE ARM'S SWING — it stands 10..13 cm to
       the arm's right, in line with its base — and it is a real stop on the rig (the tutorial's
       F8: the calibration span guard flagged `shoulder_pan` on ONE side,
       the tower's). Do not remove it to make a task easier. -->
  <compiler angle="radian" meshdir="so101_tower_stand"/>
  <asset>
    <mesh name="arm_base" file="arm_base.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="cam_mount_bottom" file="cam_mount_bottom.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="cam_mount_middle" file="cam_mount_middle.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="cam_mount_top" file="cam_mount_top.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <material name="stand_pla" rgba="0.20 0.45 0.85 1"/>
  </asset>
  <worldbody>
    <body name="stand">
      <!-- visual: the four prints, in the bottom part's frame -->
      <!-- arm_base: the foot's orientation, chained onto its z = 0 end; the
           plate spans body x 0..90, y 0..159 -->
      <geom type="mesh" mesh="arm_base" material="stand_pla" contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="2"
            pos="0.094 0.026 0.005" quat="0.7071068 0.7071068 0 0"/>
      <geom type="mesh" mesh="cam_mount_bottom" material="stand_pla" contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="2"
            pos="0 0 0" quat="0.7071068 0.7071068 0 0"/>
      <geom type="mesh" mesh="cam_mount_middle" material="stand_pla" contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="2"
            pos="0.0187 -0.0365 0.188" quat="0.7071068 0.7071068 0 0"/>
      <geom type="mesh" mesh="cam_mount_top" material="stand_pla" contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="2"
            pos="0.0187 -0.0365 0.188" quat="0.7071068 0.7071068 0 0"/>
      <!-- collision: foot, post, head, base plate -->
      <geom name="foot" type="box" pos="0.0187 -0.0365 0.005" size="0.0187 0.0465 0.005"
            rgba="0.20 0.45 0.85 0.3" group="3"/>
      <geom name="post" type="box" pos="0.0187 -0.0365 0.277" size="0.0127 0.0183 0.265"
            rgba="0.20 0.45 0.85 0.3" group="3"/>
      <geom name="head" type="box" pos="0.0517 -0.0365 0.551" size="0.012 0.0175 0.009"
            rgba="0.20 0.45 0.85 0.3" group="3"/>
      <geom name="plate" type="box" pos="0.045 0.0795 0.0025" size="0.045 0.0795 0.0025"
            rgba="0.20 0.45 0.85 0.3" group="3"/>
      <!-- the overhead IMX335, CALIBRATED — see the header -->
      <camera name="overhead_cam" pos="0.06067 -0.03998 0.53666"
              xyaxes="0.0059 -1.0000 0.0069  0.9042 0.0083 0.4270" fovy="73.7398"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
